1
00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.433
Welcome to this short course.

2
00:00:01.433 --> 00:00:05.600
Built in partnership with Nexusflow
and taught by Jiantao Jiao,

3
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.333
who is the co-founder and CEO of Nexusflow,
as well as assistant

4
00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:12.400
professor of ESS stats at UC Berkeley.

5
00:00:12.666 --> 00:00:16.333
And Venkat Srinivasan,
who is a founding engineer at Nexusflow.

6
00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:19.000
Large language models
are trained on text,

7
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:23.200
which is a form of unstructured data,
but our computing infrastructure

8
00:00:23.466 --> 00:00:27.600
is largely built on structured data
with virtually defined interfaces

9
00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:31.366
like APIs
that expect data in a certain format.

10
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.100
Function-calling bridges this gap.

11
00:00:35.166 --> 00:00:38.166
Here's how LLMs and function-calling works.

12
00:00:38.433 --> 00:00:43.200
In the prompt sent to a function-calling
capable LLM,

13
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:48.100
will include descriptions of functions
that are available to the LLM to use.

14
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:53.333
These descriptions include texts
describing what a function might do

15
00:00:53.666 --> 00:00:56.666
so the LLM knows
when they should use that function,

16
00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.933
as well as additionally information
needed to call that function,

17
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.666
such as that function's name
and a description of its arguments.

18
00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:10.266
When the LLM has a query that it determines
is best served by calling a function,

19
00:01:10.566 --> 00:01:14.366
the LLM will then generate
the needed parameters from the query

20
00:01:14.666 --> 00:01:17.666
and return a string
that could be used to call that function.

21
00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:21.466
Notice that the LLM doesn't
call the function directly,

22
00:01:21.466 --> 00:01:25.400
it just returns a string
that could be used to call that function.

23
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.433
These functions are often referred
to as tools,

24
00:01:28.433 --> 00:01:31.966
and can be used to extend
the capabilities of a chatbot,

25
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.033
or to build agents, for example,
such as a research agent

26
00:01:36.066 --> 00:01:40.566
whose tools might include web search
or Wikipedia lookups.

27
00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:44.933
But function-calling has proven useful
in many applications

28
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:46.800
that goes even beyond chat.

29
00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.800
For example, DeepLearning.AI
uses simple AI agents that we build

30
00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:56.966
internally to analyze learner feedback
to keep on improving our courses.

31
00:01:57.366 --> 00:02:00.366
Of course,
our core team does read your feedback,

32
00:02:00.366 --> 00:02:05.366
and we appreciate you taking the time
to provide it. And to gather statistics,

33
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.966
we provide an LLM with a prompt
that includes

34
00:02:08.966 --> 00:02:12.966
a description of a function called "record
learner feedback".

35
00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:18.200
They can record the sentiment and rating
as well as report any technical problems

36
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.666
described. The LLM that we used for
this is actually a special purpose

37
00:02:22.666 --> 00:02:26.133
LLM called NexusRavenV2-13B

38
00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.700
fine-tuned for function-
calling by Nexusflow.

39
00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.100
Andrew, I'm glad to hear that.

40
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.666
NexusRavenV2-13B

41
00:02:34.666 --> 00:02:37.966
is an open-source model
you can download from HuggingFace.

42
00:02:38.466 --> 00:02:41.600
You can also use a hosted version
available on our sites.

43
00:02:42.366 --> 00:02:45.366
It is a model
you'll be using in this course.

44
00:02:45.566 --> 00:02:47.733
Many applications do not require

45
00:02:47.733 --> 00:02:50.766
a full capability
of a general-purpose foundation model.

46
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:55.166
Next, NexusRavenV2-13B
has only a certain billion parameters,

47
00:02:55.533 --> 00:02:59.166
but can output Chat GPT-4 in some function
calling benchmarks.

48
00:03:00.066 --> 00:03:03.066
This NexusRavenV2-13B
and other fine-tuning

49
00:03:03.066 --> 00:03:06.400
smaller models,
are small enough to be locally hosted.

50
00:03:06.966 --> 00:03:11.000
This eliminates the latency
and cost barriers that might prevent you

51
00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.300
from adding a natural language interface
to your applications.

52
00:03:15.166 --> 00:03:19.200
There are many instances where you may
want to convert natural language inputs,

53
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.400
into structured outputs,
like a function call.

54
00:03:22.966 --> 00:03:26.433
Your business may have a library
of functions that perform dashboard

55
00:03:26.433 --> 00:03:30.366
operations, and you would like to add
a natural language interface.

56
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:35.000
Or you might have applications
that need to process transcripts,

57
00:03:35.333 --> 00:03:38.766
notes, or proceedings
and store them in a database.

58
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:42.566
Every business has its own unique
applications.

59
00:03:42.566 --> 00:03:45.566
In this course, Venkat will be showing you

60
00:03:45.666 --> 00:03:48.966
how you can add a function-calling capability
to your applications.

61
00:03:49.566 --> 00:03:52.000
We'll start by taking a deeper dive into

62
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.000
what function-calling is
and how you can use it.

63
00:03:55.133 --> 00:03:59.266
You will form prompts with function
definitions, as Andrew described,

64
00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:03.200
and then you'll use the LLM response
to call those functions.

65
00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.400
Once you have mastered that,
we'll kick things up a notch,

66
00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:10.400
by defining and calling
multiple functions, you will call nested

67
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.066
functions, where arguments to one function
are themselves functions.

68
00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:20.900
Many services on the web have APIs defined
using an open API description.

69
00:04:21.266 --> 00:04:25.066
You'll also learn to convert
these specifications into functions

70
00:04:25.066 --> 00:04:29.700
callable by your LLM. You will finish
the course with a practical application

71
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.733
which takes a customer's service,
transcripts

72
00:04:32.733 --> 00:04:37.400
and builds SQL calls to store
selected data to a database.

73
00:04:38.066 --> 00:04:40.566
Many people have worked to create this
course.

74
00:04:40.566 --> 00:04:45.533
I'd like to thank from Nexusflow,
Jian Zhang and Banghua Zhu. From DeepLearning.AI

75
00:04:45.666 --> 00:04:50.500
Geoff Ladwig and Esmaeil Gargari,
have also contributed to this course.

76
00:04:50.733 --> 00:04:54.166
Lots of exciting things
with function-calling in this course.

77
00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:59.333
Let's go on to the next video
to get started, and I hope this course

78
00:04:59.666 --> 00:05:03.866
will help you make your applications
highly functional.

